      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical aspect of the pitot-static system.\n\nThe pitot-static system measures airspeed by comparing the static pressure (the pressure of the air outside the aircraft) to the dynamic pressure (the pressure created by the air flowing over the pitot tube). The dynamic pressure is proportional to the square of the airspeed, while the static pressure is proportional to the airspeed.\n\nThe pitot tube is typically connected to a pitot-static probe, which consists of a pitot tube and a static port. The pitot tube is exposed to the dynamic pressure, while the static port is exposed to the static pressure. The pressure difference between the two is then measured by a pressure sensor, usually a piezoelectric or capacitive sensor.\n\nThe pressure sensor sends the pressure readings to an airspeed indicator, which uses the pressure difference to calculate the airspeed. The airspeed indicator is usually calibrated to read the pressure difference and convert it to a velocity reading.\n\nIn a typical pitot-static system, the pitot tube is usually protected from the elements by a fairing or a cowling, and the static port is usually located on the side of the aircraft. The",
